Organization = {IMIA Working Grp Human Factors Engn Healthcare Informat Org \& Social
   Issues; Hlth Informat Patient Safety; EFMI WG Human \& Org Factors Med
   Informat},
Abstract = {In previous work we have described the creation and user testing of a
   drag/drop user-composable electronic health record, MedWISE. Any new
   design poses new potential problems; here we discuss the accuracy,
   potential for new types of errors, and user reactions to the approach,
   including their perceptions of ease of use and usefulness. Our results
   come from a mixed methods laboratory study with 18 clinicians at a large
   academic medical center. 12 used MedWISE and 6 used the conventional
   system.
   MedWISE users had comparable assessment accuracy to use of the
   conventional system, low (1/10) risk of diagnosis momentum error, and
   overall favorable and even enthusiastic user perception. 10/12 said the
   system eased their cognitive process, Ease of use and usefulness were
   rated 3.79 and 4.00 on a 5-point Likert scale. Users were unconcerned
   about the possibility of increased errors due to their trust in
   colleagues and system similarities to current practice. After first
   describing the issues, we suggest a method to elucidate risks in
   innovative and current systems.},
