   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {Topic: A study of current literature focusing on modern ways of
   remedying visual perception loss from the perspective of a special
   education teacher.
   In recent years, the number of new devices that use individual parts
   implanted in the eyes or brain of a human with a visual impairment and
   which restore visual perception has been rapidly increasing. These
   people then need comprehensive rehabilitation to know how to use these
   devices properly and follow the principles of working with them. With
   the change of clients some special education teacher's tasks change too
   and are further discussed in the article.
   Method: This article is a review of journal posts from 2008-2013,
   selected to submit a brief overview of modern methods and devices
   designed to recover visual perception loss. The use of these devices is
   further connected with modern special education and special education
   teacher's work.
   Results: The study of the literature revealed that in modern age there
   are many electronic devices, mostly implants, which restore visual
   perception of blind people. Although the images obtained using these
   devices are not nearly as good as the normal sight, there is a big
   assumption that they are going to be used in increasingly large
   quantities. A special education teacher should thus not only be able to
   work with people with these implants, but also should be an integral
   part of the treatment team. As an expert who understands life of people
   with visual impairment, they could help finding suitable candidates for
   medical trials by offering adequate information not only of a medical
   nature but mainly in terms of the specifics of the usage of the devices
   in everyday life and information about the development of possible
   complications.},
