   Portuguese-Society-of-Occupational-Safety-and-Hygiene(SHO), Guimaraes,
   PORTUGAL, MAR 23-24, 2016},
Organization = {Portuguese Soc Occupat Safety \& Hygiene; Univ Minho, Sch Engn; Univ
   Porto, Fac Engn; Univ Lisbon, Fac Human Kinet; Polytechn Univ Catalonia;
   Tech Univ Delft; Faculdade Motricidade Humana; DELTA; EXIM; ACT;
   European Agcy Safety \& Hlth Work; Lavoro},
Abstract = {Noise research in hospitals is focused on patients' exposure. However
   also health professionals are exposed to disturbing levels of noise
   during a workday. This study aim to identify health staff perceptions of
   background noise levels and its impact on working environment of three
   Portuguese NICU. To achieve the main goal, a questionnaire to assess
   noise perception of professionals (N = 95) was developed and applied.
   The equivalent continuous A-weighted sound pressure levels (L-Aeq), were
   also measured and took into account the layout of unit and location of
   the main sources of noise. Health staff classified the environment
   (regarding noise) as ``slightly uncomfortable{''} (64.9\%). Although,
   48.4\% considered it as ``acceptable{''}. The majority (55.5\%)
   considered ``equipment{''} as the most annoying source of noise. In all
   NICU, noise levels are above those recommended by the WHO. The results
   provided useful information to define strategies to reduce noise and
   make the staff involved in the process.},
