Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Based on the observation that care pathway organization in France is
   complex and asymmetrical between stakeholders as pluri-professional
   caregivers (medical staff, nurses, etc.) and care receivers (patients,
   family helpers), we tried to replace that specific French organizational
   context in an acceptance projection of multi-user interactions through
   the technology itself. By interviewing fourteen French participants
   during six months respectively at work or at home - depending on each
   user profile, among both patients and caregivers, we deeply examine
   their perception of what kind of more collaborative information
   technology (IT) features could be helpful. Thus, our research intends to
   analyze what key functionalities appear as more significantly
   collaborative compared to others and doing so, trying to find a better
   understanding, classification, and measurement of multiuser interaction
   in healthcare services using innovative technology. Focusing on the
   study of multi-user interaction impacting technology acceptance, we find
   new perspectives for prototyping such collaborative tool in e-health
   services to better fit and predict technology acceptability in this
   medical context which, to some extends, highlights contradictory
   requirement needs for multi-users.},
