   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {An approach to increase acceptance of automated vehicles (AVs) using
   emotional eyes of AVs for pedestrian-AV interaction is presented. In an
   online survey conducted in Germany with 245 participants, key design
   dimensions for an abstract emotional eye design and the acceptance of
   AVs expressing Ekman's six basic emotions in pedestrian-AV interactions
   were examined. Results show that the design dimensions of shape, color
   and motion enhance emotion recognition in abstract eye designs, with
   specific dimensions being more effective for certain emotions. While
   Disgust is the least, Anger and Happiness are the most accepted emotions
   of AVs by pedestrians. Beyond the attitudes toward AV-expressed emotions
   studied here, the findings indicate that emotionalizing AVs influences
   pedestrians' affective and behavioral outcomes in interactions,
   warranting further studies in more ecologically valid settings.},
