   26-28, 2020},
Organization = {Yildiz Tech Univ, Environm Engn Dept; ISTAC Inc; Int Waste Working Grp;
   T C Cevre Sehircili Bakanligi; Turkish Airlines; Istanbul Convent \&
   Visitors Bur; Cevre Norm; Artemis Aritim; Eced Cevre Danismanlik
   Muhendislik Hizmetleri San Ve Tic Ltd Sti; Ciftligim Gubre; MSB ARGE
   Cevre Enerji Teknolojileri Muh Egitim Dans Ltd Sti; Tin Muhendislik;
   Muhendislik Ar Ge Egitim \& Danismanlik Ltd Sti; Aktan; Galaksi},
Abstract = {Unprecedented population growth and ever increasing economic activities
   in almost lower middle income group countries of the world like India
   are facing the problem of solid waste disposal, typically landfilling in
   open low lying areas is still the common method for solid waste
   management in such countries. Availability of the land at outskirts of
   large urban area is also a crucial issue for waste disposal due to
   protest of local people. Now-a-days geospatial data and GIS technique,
   is a vital tool and extremely popular approach to overcome and solve the
   problem of environmental contamination due to solid waste. This study
   involve the use of Resourcesat-2 LISS-III 23.5m spatial resolution data
   and ground truth data for creation of multi-criterion layers i.e.
   landuse/cover, surface waterbodies, habitation/residential areas, soil
   texture, lithology, ground water prospects and geomorphology and
   road/transport network and these layers are assigned the weightage
   between one to nine in Geographical Information System (GIS)
   environment. In study area four landfill sites are identified and
   marked. This study is an outcome of World Bank funded project through U.
   P. Jal Nigam. This paper presents the results on selection of suitable
   landfill site based on geospatial data, creation of buffer and weighted
   overlay analysis in GIS environment, is an endeavor to select the most
   suitable site for Hardoi city and its surroundings local bodies of Uttar
   Pradesh, India.},
