   Chemistry Project (IGAC), BOLOGNA, ITALY, SEP 13-17, 1999},
Organization = {IGAC; CNR, Inst Atmospher \& Ocean Sci; European Commiss Res DG; Joint
   Res Ctr; Commiss Atmospher Chem \& Global Pollut; Regione Emilia
   Romagna; Rolo Banca 1473; Univ Bologna; US Natl Sci Fdn, Atmospher Chem
   Program; World Meterol Org},
Abstract = {The present study reports the pH and chemical composition of rain water
   at four urban sites of Delhi and one rural site away from Delhi. The
   collected samples were analysed for major cations (Ca, K, Mg, Na and
   NH3) and anions (SO4, NO3 and Cl). Data showed very high pH and Ca
   concentration at rural site which indicated the direct influence of
   crustal sources on rain water. It was estimated that a significant
   fraction of SO4 in rain water is contributed by crustal sources in this
   region. The association of high pH and high crustal SO4 suggested that
   crustal SO4 is not responsible for acidity or lowering the pH of rain
   water in India. Ion balance and conductivity balance for data qualify
   assessment was observed to be within USEPA recommended limits. (C) 2000
   Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.},
